[Applause]  we  are free people chance this crowd during  the coronavirus pandemic they say  wearing a mask and adhering to  restrictions is violating that freedom  and it seems thousands agree a mixture  of conspiracy theorists far-right  figures and citizens concerned by the  economic effects of the lockdown  took to the streets of berlin to say the  coronavirus has been a false alarm  that's just plain scare tactics i don't  see any danger  i don't know any sick people i knew many  sick people in march  skiers vacationers there was really  something going on in february  but now there's no sick people anymore  i've got a friend who is 93 she's in a  care home  in her room totally isolated there's no  corona in her home  and yet she wasn't allowed to leave her  room  now she's allowed to go out for a walk  again but if she meets someone  she has to give their address i think  that's extreme  our demand is back to democracy get rid  of the laws that restrict us  get rid of the masks that turn us into  slaves  hardly any of the demonstrators were  wearing face masks or adhering to social  distancing protocols  berlin police attempted to enforce  distancing measures over megaphones  eventually dissolving the protests and  telling people to go home  german politicians mostly condemned the  demonstration  saying that it risks harming germany's  relative success at handling the  outbreak  that success might just be why these  people feel impatient to get back to  normal life  but with a fresh spike in coveted cases  across the country  a gathering of thousands of people not  wearing masks  could jeopardize the freedoms germans  are enjoying compared to many other  countries  hit by the pandemic  well with me in the studio now is dw  political correspondent leonie von  hammerstein  who is at the protest mooney you are out  there for hours  you told me um you spoke to lots of  people there  what were they telling you what were  their demands  well there were a lot of different  people out there and as you said we  spoke to many of them  some of their demands included including  by the organizers who  who put their demands in the document  before the event i'm saying  calling on that that was the end of the  pandemic they called this the day of  freedom  and they then called on the german  government to resign  and on the state the public broadcasters  so the media to also be dissolved and  these were the three demands that were  echoed by many people who i spoke to at  the protest  and what united them was a distrust in  the government's measures and  we have to remember this is kind of a  paradox you said it just now  and a lot of the people were telling me  well and the government was telling us  that we have to  keep our distance and stick to all these  restrictions  in order for the hospitals not to be  overcrowded in order for people not to  die  but nobody died not that many people  died the hospitals are still empty  and so what did the government tell us  in the first place  and this is why this is this inherent  distrust in what the government is  telling them  and that's why you saw i saw today  thousands tens of thousands of people  take to the streets  not adhering to the rules not adhering  to the face masks  and they kind of use a lot of them were  telling me they they see the face mask  as sort of that symbol of oppression  that the government is forcing them to  wear masks i mean we also have to  remember germany has released a lot of  its measures a lot of its restrictions  and in recent weeks which has as you  said we've seen a search in  new infections so enormous amounts of  distrust of the government but who  are these people loney i mean i'm  wondering because lots of people are  quite grateful to the german government  for its swift response to the pandemic  and the measures that it implemented  they're grateful that it saved lives but  these people who  don't believe that who are they well  they're also  um you can't there's a wide variety of  people who attended these um  protests today and what we can say  some people were talking to me there  were people who were worried about the  economic consequences of the pandemic  there was a bus driver from croatia who  told me  well i haven't been able to run my  business for four months how should i  survive  there have been people who are worried  about their children not being able to  go back to school  which they are but the education system  being changed i'm  worried about what this pandemic will  leave what kind of country  will it leave to the children but we  also have to say that  um far-right groups have been mobilizing  for this protest not only the afd  the party that sits in the biggest  opposition party in the german bundestag  also the npd  um compact magazine magazine which is a  far-right magazine that's under the  observation of the authorities  for example and revisionist messages  conspiracy theorists i talked to some  people who  believed um that bill gates wanted to um  spread to vaccinate the whole the whole  world so really these conspiracy  theories that we are not only seeing in  germany but also  across the world for where people um use  this pandemic to feed into these  conspiracy theories  a lot of them also anti-semitic  backgrounds we saw  um some placards there today so it's  hard to say i i'd say the majority was  peaceful but i'd also say we got a lot  of verbal  abuse from protesters who were calling  us liars  and we were calling um as ludenpresser  so you know  lying press i mean that's echoing nazi  terminology is it not  yes exactly leonie von hammerstein thank  you so much for your reporting and for  sharing your impressions with us in the  studio thank you

